Crop Recording | Ubuntu X11 (Ubuntu on Xorg)

After installation, Crop Recording runs as a service in your X11 desktop session.
The installer starts it for users already logged in to X11, and the desktop
starts it at future logins.

When upgrading from the old croprec package, the new package replaces it.

1. Press Ctrl+Enter to open the area picker and its on-screen instructions.
2. Drag to select the area to record. Press Esc to cancel.
3. Press Ctrl+Enter to record. The rest of the screen is dimmed and shows
   the elapsed time. Leave enough space outside the selection for the timer.
4. Press Ctrl+Enter again to stop. In the dialog, choose Save to Folder or Copy Video.

To run from source without installing Crop Recording, launch it in an X11 session
with the dependencies in DEBIAN/control available:

  /usr/bin/python3 usr/bin/crop-recording
  /usr/bin/python3 usr/bin/crop-recording --region 120,80,640,481

The --region option preselects an area when the picker opens. Ctrl+C exits
the foreground process. For the installed service, use
systemctl --user status crop-recording.service to check its status.

Crop Recording records the chosen pixels and the cursor at 30 fps, with no audio,
using lossless RGB H.264 in an MP4. Some players require software decoding.
The overlay stays outside the video. On an X11 desktop without a compositor,
the dimmed area uses a fine stipple so the desktop remains visible.

Save to Folder opens the desktop file picker. Copy Video offers the MP4
as a file attachment, with no plain-text clipboard format. Clips up to
32 MiB are also offered as video/mp4 data. Copies are in ~/Crop Recording Clipboard
so confined browsers can read them. Keep the source file there while the
receiving app is using it. A website must support pasting attachments to
accept Ctrl+V. Otherwise, select the MP4 through its upload dialog.
The resulting path is printed to the service log or terminal.
